  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Dry conditions and seasonable temperatures \nexpected this weekend and into next week. \n\nTonight: High pressure will extend across the region tonight. A\nbit of a pressure gradient between higher pressure to the north\nand weak inverted northwest-southeast oriented surface trough \nover Georgia will likely keep the winds up a bit slightly this \nevening before going light/calm after midnight as the boundary \nlayer decouples. High clouds associated with the subtropical jet\nwill slowly thin out with time with mostly clear conditions \nexpected late. This will allow some modest radiational cooling \nto occur, especially over the South Carolina Lowcountry where a\nlonger boundary decoupling is expected to occur. Lows will \nrange from the mid-upper 40s inland to the upper 50s/near 60 at \nthe beaches. Some lower 40s are possible, mainly over central \nand northern portions of Berkeley County and interior upper \nCharleston County in the Francis Marion National Forest. \n\nSunday through Saturday: Surface High pressure will prevail \nacross the Southeast U.S. the first half of next week, bringing \ndry conditions. High temperatures Sunday should range from \naround 70 degrees to the mid 70s, except cooler at the beaches. \nTemperatures rise a few degrees on Monday, peaking in the mid to\nupper 70s, which is a few degrees above normal. These highs \nshould again be reached Tuesday and Wednesday. The combination \nof light to calm winds and mostly clear skies should lead to \ndecent radiational cooling Sunday and Monday nights. Lows should\ndip to around 40 degrees far inland, and the 50s at/near the \nbeaches. An increase in clouds and perhaps winds should warm up \nlow temperatures a few degrees Tuesday and Wednesday nights.\n\nA fast moving cold front could bring a brief period of light \nshowers on Thursday. Behind the front, expect dry conditions on \nFriday along with temperatures well below normal, especially \novernights.",
